{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 29, :comments ["<b>And you shall make.</b> What the Rabbis said is well-known." "<b>And the word אשר יסך בהן [with which he pours with them].</b> Its alef and qashwot refer not to the one near it, which is the menaqiyyot, as in משה ואהרן בכהניו ושמואל בקוראי שמו (Psalms 99:6), and it is written in the pillar of cloud ידבר אליהם (ibid. 7), which are Moses and Aaron, not Samuel. And the proof is that in the account he mentioned the qashwot, אשר יסך בהן (below, 37:16), and furthermore, ואת קשות הנסך (Numbers 4:7). And the word נסך has many meanings: לא יסכו לה נסך (Hosea 9:4), הפסל נסך חרש (Isaiah 40:19), ואני נסכתי מלכי (Psalms 2:6). Said Rabbi Nathan, who is the author of the Arukh, that the qashwot are in the shape of reeds, for so they are called in the Ishmaelite tongue. And the one who told him this did not know Arabic, for he was a laughingstock, for reeds are called qatzav with a tzadi, also in the Temple. And there is a confusion in Chronicles, where he mentioned mizraqim in place of the qearot, and kappurim in place of the kappot, and he mentioned the qashwot as they are, and in place of the menaqiyyot he said mizlagot. And all are gold for the needs of the table. Perhaps they were other vessels that David commanded to place on the tables that Solomon would make."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {1 "Ibn Ezra refutes the Arukh's Arabic etymology; the Ishmaelite word for reeds uses tzadi (קצב), not samekh (קשות). The referent of 'he mentioned' and 'he said' in the Chronicles discussion (likely the Chronicler) is left ambiguous as in the original."}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:38:30.674050868Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}